#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 720.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120621} :activity/id #uuid "1cf102a8-b50b-50b9-b9e1-ba6d06bcd005" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048505 :topic/id #uuid "a564c0d5-78a2-53e7-86da-2f6c443f5501" :topic/math-academy-id 1770} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120621 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121018 :tutorial/content "Sometimes there are lots of calculations involved in finding the determinant of a matrix using a cofactor expansion across the first row. \n\nFortunately, however, we don't always have to use the first row! We can use *any* row we like. A cofactor expansion across any general row is known as a **Laplace expansion**.\n\nAcross the row $i,$ the Laplace expansion of an $n\\!\\times\\! n$ matrix $A$ is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & ={\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}{a}_{ij}{C}_{ij} \\\\  & ={a}_{i1}{C}_{i1}+{a}_{i2}{C}_{i2}+⋯+{a}_{in}{C}_{in},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\color{blue}C_{ij}}$ is the cofactor of the entry $\\color{red}a_{ij}$ in $A.$ Remember the cofactor of the entry $\\color{red}a_{ij}$ is \n\n$$\n{\\color{black}C_{ij}}=(-1)^{i+j}{\\color{black}M_{ij}},\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\color{black}M_{ij}}$ is the minor of the entry.\n\nFor example, consider the following matrix:\n\n<div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$B=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>\n\nTo compute the determinant of the matrix above, it is most convenient to use a Laplace expansion along the row with the most zeros. So, let's use Laplace expansion across the third row ($i=3$) as follows:\n\n![](images/6e/6e8d5f7f4111e8963a6d4c7daf2bd5116d8099e18f41c53f2dae73f659f01e35.svg)\n\nSo, we reduced a $4 \\times 4$ determinant to a $3 \\times 3$ determinant." :tutorial/id #uuid "e9ee666a-da25-5521-b0bf-5d90a89b6e5d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3001 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "cd1eda6c-ca73-5cc1-91b6-a6fdef05cb05" :step/math-academy-id 16206 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120730}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120622 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119773 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119587 :question/id #uuid "8235213e-4e88-57f6-89b0-de589ad2b38d" :question/math-academy-id "e-4418"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d1637e23-6566-5e45-af4c-b27333f882d1" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120424} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120425} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338749} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338750} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338751} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338752} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345306} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345307} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345337} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345338} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369686} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370933} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371077} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425353}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Determinant Using a Laplace Expansion Across a Row"} :step/id #uuid "c97ef0ce-8fe0-5462-8edf-ea6b6fce37f7" :step/math-academy-id 16207 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120623}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120623 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121019 :tutorial/content "We can also use the Laplace expansion across any *column* to find the determinant. Across the column $j,$ the Laplace expansion is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{a}_{ij}{C}_{ij} \\\\  & ={a}_{1j}{C}_{1j}+{a}_{2j}{C}_{2j}+⋯+{a}_{nj}{C}_{nj},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\color{blue}C_{ij}$ is the cofactor of the entry ${\\color{red}a_{ij}}.$\n\nIn other words, the Laplace expansion across a row or column is the sum of the entries in the row or column multiplied by their respective cofactors.\n\nTo find the determinant of a matrix, we can choose to use the Laplace expansion across any row or column, so choose the row or column that has the most zeros, to cut down on the calculations!" :tutorial/id #uuid "3c3bdfc6-9f31-558b-9e75-725b2a92027f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3002 :tutorial/title "Laplace Expansion Across a Column"} :step/id #uuid "678f4e20-a38c-51ea-909d-5d7d1577436d" :step/math-academy-id 16208 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120624}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120624 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119774 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119588 :question/id #uuid "6f0a6499-03db-5833-b175-4835325d34d6" :question/math-academy-id "e-4419"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "db6f57f3-d9f0-5ea7-a557-e729a8c0777f" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120404} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120423} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265821} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323769} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326927} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338731} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345311} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345341} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345342} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369693} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370926} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392468} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393709} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393716} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425352}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Determinant Using a Laplace Expansion Across a Column"} :step/id #uuid "bb444283-a8b6-5668-8558-32d154a2839b" :step/math-academy-id 16209} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120730 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119843 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119657 :question/id #uuid "5cc1443c-03cd-5f86-bcfc-b9b7f4c6a565" :question/math-academy-id "e-5182"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a88f6712-2115-55ce-abe4-b3c27e2e105f" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050092}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120402} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120403} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265802} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186336026} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345305} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345335} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345336} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392973} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425877}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying a Laplace Expansion Across a Row"} :step/id #uuid "ada1363c-ec10-55ea-a2b2-e872dcb901ad" :step/math-academy-id 19018 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120622}}] :activity/title "Finding Determinants Using Laplace Expansions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186119992}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425355} :activity/id #uuid "3deab7c7-601d-5aa7-a706-f09271ec407c" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048505 :topic/id #uuid "a564c0d5-78a2-53e7-86da-2f6c443f5501" :topic/math-academy-id 1770} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425354 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425352 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e448fea-5855-52bc-a7dd-48d77cb4df08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 5 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 8 & -9 & 6\\end{aligned}|-7\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 5 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 4 & 6 & 5\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425340} {:answer/id #uuid "573033dd-3b24-588e-815d-f15a4d5a4ed1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 5 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 8 & -9 & 6\\end{aligned}|+7\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 5 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 4 & 6 & 5\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425342} {:answer/id #uuid "a302af15-3d2f-5841-a490-4042a6be47b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-7\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 5 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 4 & 6 & 5\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425345} {:answer/id #uuid "cd4a358d-1348-54aa-b944-266d98876cd0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 5 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 8 & -9 & 6\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425347} {:answer/id #uuid "ea7ef906-9479-54e2-b634-98c804680ba2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-7\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 5 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 8 & -9 & 6\\end{aligned}|-3\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 5 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 4 & 6 & 5\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425348}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425342} :answer-field/id #uuid "ed2a715c-bbc0-55b9-9770-63cc37682a33" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425351}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "cd765ff8-911d-50bf-a599-8cf7e9df87e6" :question/math-academy-id "q-77807" :question/problem "$|\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 3 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 6 & 5 & -3 \\\\ 8 & -9 & 6 & 7\\end{aligned}|=$" :question/worked-solution "Using a cofactor expansion along the $4$th column, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 3 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 6 & 5 & -3 \\\\ 8 & -9 & 6 & 7\\end{aligned}| & =0\\cdot {C}_{14}+0\\cdot {C}_{24}+(-3)\\cdot {C}_{34}+7\\cdot {C}_{44} \\\\  & =(-3)\\cdot (-1)^{3+4}|\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 5 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 8 & -9 & 6\\end{aligned}|+7\\cdot (-1)^{4+4}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 5 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 4 & 6 & 5\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 5 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 8 & -9 & 6\\end{aligned}|+7\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 5 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 4 & 6 & 5\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "0b0ff6e1-7449-568f-b593-44cc09974a71" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425356}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425355 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338752 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "45ac0f97-eeb8-5783-936b-1cb59033a9ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336679} {:answer/id #uuid "4b236f4f-c6e5-553f-9345-4b6a8bba7c7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336725} {:answer/id #uuid "6029e681-c7c2-5a8b-96eb-6ad30cbe83ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336903} {:answer/id #uuid "72576609-7b8d-50eb-8ffa-8aef7ffc7d00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337056} {:answer/id #uuid "862a7584-abcb-5134-9117-8e928e486ca6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337244}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186336679} :answer-field/id #uuid "907db199-a612-5898-a313-2594bace9c74" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338542}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "fa886c44-6fbc-583a-a5aa-8d0e7dbb66ff" :question/math-academy-id "q-70133" :question/problem "Calculate\n$|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}|.$" :question/worked-solution "The $4$th row of the given matrix contains two zeros, more than any other row. So, using a Laplace expansion along the $4$th row, we have   \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}| & =2\\cdot {C}_{41}+0\\cdot {C}_{42}+0\\cdot {C}_{43}+3\\cdot {C}_{44} \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-1)^{4+1}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|+3\\cdot (-1)^{4+4}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2|\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|+3|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the first $3\\times 3$  determinant by applying a Laplace expansion along the $2$nd row, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| & =(-1)\\cdot {C}_{21}+2\\cdot {C}_{22}+0\\cdot {C}_{23} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot (-1)^{2+1}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|+2\\cdot (-1)^{2+2}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|+2|\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-2-3)+2(1-3) \\\\  & =-5+2\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =-9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute the second $3\\times 3$ determinant by applying a Laplace expansion along its $3$rd row:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| & =0\\cdot {C}_{31}+1\\cdot {C}_{32}+1\\cdot {C}_{33} \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1)^{3+2}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}|+1\\cdot (-1)^{3+3}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-|\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}|+|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-(4-2)+(-2+1) \\\\  & =-2+(-1) \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}|=-2\\cdot (-9)+3\\cdot (-3)=18-9=9.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "37d0a7e2-99c5-5b88-87e2-cc62f6bed604" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425358}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425356 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345342 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5cf7cfbc-f2b0-5684-bbfc-df2a4dfe1cd3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-8\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 6 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -9 & 4\\end{aligned}|+7\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 5 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 6 & -2\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343018} {:answer/id #uuid "6745fcba-99f1-5578-83b5-4b228c41d687" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-7\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 6 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -9 & 4\\end{aligned}|-8\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 5 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 6 & -2\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343115} {:answer/id #uuid "96c34368-3ce8-50f2-93cc-27f082bae5c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-8\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 5 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 6 & -2\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343556} {:answer/id #uuid "a6f5b999-091a-5e45-b917-5e6ca067d8b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 6 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -9 & 4\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343707} {:answer/id #uuid "bf449a53-c16e-5f60-859a-2068ef5af47d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 6 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -9 & 4\\end{aligned}|-8\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 5 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 6 & -2\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343918}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186343115} :answer-field/id #uuid "f64fee77-2b23-5772-9fa3-c06da3bf3384" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344924}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "82b9ddc1-7a19-5213-af13-fb4eb5bd3ace" :question/math-academy-id "q-77808" :question/problem "$|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -4 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -7 & 5 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 6 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -8 & -9 & 4\\end{aligned}|=$" :question/worked-solution "Using a cofactor expansion along the $2$nd column, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -4 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -7 & 5 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 6 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -8 & -9 & 4\\end{aligned}| & =0\\cdot {C}_{12}+(-7)\\cdot {C}_{22}+0\\cdot {C}_{32}+(-8)\\cdot {C}_{42} \\\\  & =(-7)\\cdot (-1)^{2+2}|\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 6 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -9 & 4\\end{aligned}|+(-8)\\cdot (-1)^{4+2}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 5 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 6 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-7\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 6 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -9 & 4\\end{aligned}|-8\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 5 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 6 & -2\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "4caaadf2-991c-5e83-b9c9-f302cb53937f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425357}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425357 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265821 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "664a8be3-c96f-5f40-a756-b75cb919d17f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 7 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -4 & -1\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263884} {:answer/id #uuid "7a1201de-9f59-557b-b8e0-5e3a27178624" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-7\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 7 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -3 & -1\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264059} {:answer/id #uuid "a56002a4-5702-52ca-a68b-b7c638eeda99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 7 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -3 & -1\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264417} {:answer/id #uuid "e5560c16-93e7-5932-afa1-a7786605d913" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 7 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -3 & -1\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264997} {:answer/id #uuid "fad808be-4742-57d4-9e6a-a4df52b11186" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3|\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & 5 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 7 \\\\ 2 & -3 & -1\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265196}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186264997} :answer-field/id #uuid "51bf7c37-6d3e-5b7f-9709-5d6988af45d7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265393}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ba53d6e2-edac-5757-8fc8-0a9f2f226683" :question/math-academy-id "q-130092" :question/problem "$|\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 & 1 & 5 \\\\ -7 & 2 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 7 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -3 & -1\\end{aligned}|=$" :question/worked-solution "Using a cofactor expansion along the $1$st column, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 & 1 & 5 \\\\ -7 & 2 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 7 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -3 & -1\\end{aligned}| & =0\\cdot {C}_{11}+-7\\cdot {C}_{21}+0\\cdot {C}_{31}+0\\cdot {C}_{41} \\\\  & =(-7)\\cdot (-1)^{2+1}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 7 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -3 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =7\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 7 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -3 & -1\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "5c12a418-c9d4-5275-a573-e37841dac7e1"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425358 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425353 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f4bae87-8e07-5dc7-9219-6f715b2ead18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "72" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425341} {:answer/id #uuid "67de8883-fc93-5260-a637-aad0f0e00eaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425343} {:answer/id #uuid "9ad5be32-123d-550b-a8af-5eb97cc44261" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "82" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425344} {:answer/id #uuid "a3bfe935-3937-5ddc-93c2-d9d7115d3c4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "90" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425346} {:answer/id #uuid "eec09744-a423-5762-9061-2c0d2667e27b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-86" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425349}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425349} :answer-field/id #uuid "836f09e9-0332-59b3-a2c7-f5f742bda069" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425350}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "f2f19b4d-d8c8-5ecf-9fc4-030f5c14f82f" :question/math-academy-id "q-77803" :question/problem "Compute the following determinant using a cofactor expansion along the $3$rd row:\n\n$$\n|\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}|.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using cofactor expansion along the $3$rd row, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}| & =0\\cdot {C}_{31}+0\\cdot {C}_{32}+4\\cdot {C}_{33}+3\\cdot {C}_{34} \\\\  & =4\\cdot (-1)^{3+3}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}|+3\\cdot (-1)^{3+4}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =4\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}|-3\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the first $3\\times 3$ determinant by applying a Laplace expansion along the $2$nd row, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}| & =0\\cdot {C}_{21}+(-2)\\cdot {C}_{22}+(-4)\\cdot {C}_{23} \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot (-1)^{2+2}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}|+(-4)\\cdot (-1)^{2+3}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}|+4\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2(6-0)+4(0-2) \\\\  & =-12-8 \\\\  & =-20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the second  $3\\times 3$ determinant by applying a Laplace expansion along the $3$rd row, we obtain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}| & =1\\cdot {C}_{31}+0\\cdot {C}_{32}+-1\\cdot {C}_{33} \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1)^{3+1}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -3\\end{aligned}|+(-1)\\cdot (-1)^{3+3}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -3\\end{aligned}|-|\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-6+2)-(-6-0) \\\\  & =-4+6 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n|\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}|=4(-20)-3(2)=-86.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "6f37f61b-9c5a-5f69-89ee-bb85a710c786" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425354}}] :activity/title "Finding Determinants Using Laplace Expansions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425339}]}
